3.text:
For a just identified homoskedastic model the Nagar first order bias can be
written, up to $o_{p}(1)$,

\begin{equation*}
\beta_{2SLS}-\beta \;=\;\frac{\sigma_{uv}}{\sigma_{v}^{2}}\;
\frac{k_{2}}{\mu^{2}}+o_{p}\!\left(\mu^{-2}\right)\ ,
\qquad 
\frac{{\rm Bias}_{2SLS}}{{\rm Bias}_{OLS}}
   \simeq\frac{k_{2}}{\mu^{2}}\;,
\end{equation*}

and with one instrument $k_{2}=1$ and, up to the usual
$n^{-1}$ factors,

\begin{equation*}
\mu^{2}= \frac{\pi^{\prime}Z^{\prime}Z\pi}{\sigma_{v}^{2}}
      \;\simeq\;F_{1},
\end{equation*}

so that in the usual first–order  approximation

\begin{equation*}
{\rm RelBias}\approx \frac{1}{F}\;,
\end{equation*}

and the first–stage $t$–ratio is $t_{F}=\sqrt{F}$.  Asymptotically

\begin{equation*}
t_{F}\;\xrightarrow{d}\;N\!\left(\delta,1\right),\qquad 
\delta=\sqrt{\mu^{2}},
\end{equation*}

so that a one–sided $95\,\%$ lower confidence bound for $\delta$ is
$t_{F}-z_{0.95}$.  With the Nagar approximation ${\rm RelBias}\simeq
\delta^{-2}$ the condition ${\rm RelBias}<0.10$ is $\delta>\sqrt{10}$.
Hence, on the normal approximation,

\begin{equation*}
t_{F}-z_{0.95}>\sqrt{10} ,
\end{equation*}

i.e. 
\begin{equation*}
t_{F}>\sqrt{10}+z_{0.95} .
\end{equation*}

With the conventional one-sided $z_{0.95}=1.645$

\begin{equation*}
t_{F}>3.162+1.645=4.81,
\qquad 
F_{0}\approx t_{F}^{2}\approx4.81^{2}\approx 23,
\end{equation*}

so that the $95\,\%$ lower bound for the concentration parameter is
$\mu_{L}^{2}\approx F_{0}-1.645\sqrt{2F_{0}}>10$, i.e. a $95\,\%$
one-sided confidence that the Nagar relative bias is below $0.10$.
In the more usual two-sided $95\,\%$ normal interval
$z_{0.975}=1.96$,

\begin{equation*}
t_{F}> \sqrt{10}+1.96\approx5.12,\qquad 
F_{0}\approx5.12^{2}\approx 26,
\end{equation*}

which is the $F\approx 24$–$26$ figure usually quoted, $F\approx23$ on the
strict one-sided $95\,\%$ normal lower bound, i.e. in round numbers
$F\gtrsim 2\!-\!3\times10^{1}$.  This is the familiar $F\gtrsim 10$ mean
bias rule inflated by the $\approx\!1.96$ standard normal safety factor.
The one-sided $95\,\%$ Nagar bound is $F\approx 2.3\times10^{1}$, 
$\approx 23$–$24$, about $26$ on the conventional two-sided $95\,\%$
$1.96$ normal calculation. ∎

9.text:
In the weak–identification scaling write $\pi_{n}=c/\sqrt n$ and put
$\mu^{2}=c^{2}q/\sigma_{v}^{2}$.  Then
\begin{equation*}
\hat\beta_{IV}-\beta=
\frac{\,Z'u\,}{Z'X}= \frac{b}{c q+a}+o_{p}(1) ,
\qquad a=\frac{Z'v}{\sqrt n},\;b=\frac{Z'u}{\sqrt n} ,
\end{equation*}
so that with the usual weak–identification normalisation
\begin{equation*}
E\big[\hat\beta_{IV}-\beta\big]\;\approx\;
-\,\frac{E[a b]}{c^{2}q^{2}}=-\,\frac{\sigma_{uv}}{c^{2}q}
=-\,\frac{\sigma_{uv}}{\sigma_{v}^{2}}\;\frac{1}{\mu^{2}} .
\end{equation*}
The OLS probability limit in the same normalisation is
$\beta_{OLS}-\beta=\sigma_{uv}/\sigma_{v}^{2}+o(1)$, so that in the
weak–identification limit
\begin{equation*}
\rho_{IV}:=\frac{{\rm bias}(\hat\beta_{IV})}
{{\rm bias}(\hat\beta_{OLS})}\approx\frac{1}{\mu^{2}}\;,
\qquad 
|\rho_{IV}|\approx \mu^{-2}.
\end{equation*}
For one excluded instrument, up to an $o_{p}(1)$ term,
\begin{equation*}
F=\frac{\hat\pi^{2}Z'Z}{\hat\sigma_{v}^{2}}\;\xrightarrow{d}\;
\chi^{2}_{1}(\mu^{2})=(Z+\sqrt{\mu^{2}})^{2},\qquad Z\sim N(0,1) ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $F$ is essentially the square of a $N(\sqrt{\mu^{2}},1)$ variable.
For $\sqrt{\mu^{2}}\gg1$ the lower $1-\alpha$ confidence bound for
$\mu^{2}$ can be written in the usual Wald form
\begin{equation*}
\mu^{2}_{L}(F)\approx\bigl(\sqrt{F}-z_{1-\alpha}\bigr)^{2},\qquad
{\rm P}\!\left\{\mu^{2}\ge\mu^{2}_{L}(F)\right\}\approx1-\alpha .
\end{equation*}
Hence a $1-\alpha$ upper bound for the Nagar relative bias is
$|\rho_{IV}|_{U}\approx \mu_{L}^{-2}(F)$.  Putting the bound at the
conventional $10\%$ level, $|\rho_{IV}|_{U}=0.10$, i.e. $\mu^{2}_{L}=10$,
gives
\begin{equation*}
\bigl(\sqrt{F_{*} }-z_{1-\alpha}\bigr)^{2}=10 ,
\end{equation*}
so that, on the normal approximation,
\begin{equation*}
\sqrt{F_{*}}\approx\sqrt{10}+z_{1-\alpha},\qquad
F_{*}\approx\bigl(\sqrt{10}+z_{1-\alpha}\bigr)^{2}.
\end{equation*}
With the usual one-sided $95\%$ normal quantile $z_{0.95}=1.64-1.65$
\begin{equation*}
F_{*}\approx\bigl(3.16+1.65\bigr)^{2}\approx 4.81^{2}\approx 2.3\times10^{1},
\end{equation*}
i.e. about $F\approx 23$, essentially the $F\approx 21$–$26$ range that
is obtained with the $1.96$ two-sided $95\%$ normal quantile,
$F_{*}\approx(\sqrt{10}+1.96)^{2}\approx26.2$.  In particular the
point Nagar approximation $|\rho_{IV}|\approx F^{-1}$ gives the familiar
$F\approx10$ for a $10\%$ point relative bias, and the $95\%$ one-sided
lower confidence bound for $\mu^{2}$ is $F\approx 23\!-\!24$, essentially
the Stock–Yogo $10\%$ relative-bias rule.  This is the usual
$F\gtrsim20\!-\!25$ requirement that is quoted as $F\gtrsim10$ in
point–estimate form.

Remark: with the two-sided $z_{0.975}=1.96$ the conventional $95\%$
two-sided bound is $F_{*}\approx (\sqrt{10}+1.96)^{2}\approx 26$, often
quoted as $F\approx 16$–$23$ in the Stock–Yogo tables. ∎

11.text:
For a just identified model the Nagar approximation for the 2SLS bias can be
written in homoskedastic form as

\begin{equation*}
E[\hat\beta_{2SLS}-\beta]\;\approx\;\frac{\sigma_{uv}}{\sigma^{2}_{v}}\;
\frac{k}{\mu^{2}},\qquad 
\mu^{2}= \frac{\pi 'Z'Z\pi}{\sigma^{2}_{v}},
\end{equation*}
so that with the usual OLS bias $\sigma_{uv}/\sigma^{2}_{v}$ the
( asymptotic ) relative bias is

\begin{equation*}
R\equiv
\frac{E[\hat\beta_{2SLS}-\beta]}{E[\hat\beta_{OLS}-\beta]}
\;\approx\;\frac{k}{\mu^{2}}\approx\frac{1}{F}\;,
\end{equation*}
$F\approx\mu^{2}/k$ being the first–stage $F$.
For $k=1$, $R\approx\mu^{-2}$ and $R<0.10$ is  $\mu^{2}>10$.

Put  $\lambda\equiv\mu^{2}$.  For $k=1$

\begin{equation*}
F=\frac{(Z'X)^{2}}{\hat\sigma^{2}_{v}Z'Z}\;\xrightarrow{d}\;
\chi^{2}_{1}(\lambda)=\bigl(Z+\sqrt{\lambda}\bigr)^{2},
\qquad Z\sim N(0,1),
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the large sample limit $F^{1/2}\!\approx\!Z+\sqrt{\lambda}$.
A one-sided $95\%$ lower confidence bound for $\lambda$ is therefore

\begin{equation*}
\sqrt{\lambda_{L}}=\sqrt{F_{obs}}-z_{0.95},
\qquad z_{0.95}=1.645,
\end{equation*}
so that $R_{U}\approx\lambda_{L}^{-1}$.  Imposing $R_{U}=0.10$, i.e.
$\lambda_{L}=10$, gives the $95\%$ lower confidence level critical value

\begin{equation*}
F_{0.95}(\lambda=10)\;\approx\;\bigl(\sqrt{10}+z_{0.95}\bigr)^{2}
      =\bigl(3.1623+1.645\bigr)^{2}\approx 2.31\times10^{1},
\end{equation*}
i.e. about $F\approx 22\!-\!24$.  With the usual normal approximation
$F\approx 1+\lambda+1.645\sqrt{2(1+2\lambda)}$ this is
$F_{0.95}\approx 1+10+1.645\sqrt{42}\approx 21.6$, the familiar
$F\gtrsim10$ point estimate becoming $F\approx 23$ once the
sampling uncertainty in the first-stage is taken into account.  The
one-sided $95\%$ bound is often quoted as $F\approx 16\!-\!23$,
about $F\approx23$ for the $1.645$ one-sided Nagar bound, essentially
the Stock–Yogo $10\%$ relative bias critical value.

Remark: using the two–sided $z_{0.975}=1.96$ bound $(s-1.96)^{2}>10$
gives $s>5.12$, i.e. $F\gtrsim 26$, the conventional $F>10$ rule
becoming $F\gtrsim 20\!-\!25$ on a $95\%$ confidence basis.

